validating restores

1. Preparation & Scoping

Before initiating a restore, I confirm what success looks like.

Identify restore objective

File-level, application-level, VM, database, or full system

Point-in-time (RPO) and urgency (RTO)

Confirm backup health

Job status, completion time, backup type (full/incremental/synthetic)

Verify no active errors (e.g., DDB issues, media errors, snapshot failures)

Choose restore method

In-place vs out-of-place

Alternate location / sandbox / DR environment

Access & change control

Confirm permissions, approvals, and maintenance windows if required

2. Restore Execution

Execution is done methodically to minimize risk.

Select correct backup copy

Primary vs secondary copy

Disk vs tape vs cloud tier

Perform restore

Use test restore first if possible (especially for Tier 1 systems)

For application-aware restores:

Ensure proper agents, credentials, and consistency options

Monitor restore job

Track throughput, media access, and error messages

Address common issues:

Network bottlenecks

Media mount delays

Permission or path conflicts

3. Validation & Verification (Most Critical Step)

A restore is not complete until it is validated.

Technical Validation

File restores

Verify file presence, size, permissions, and timestamps

VM restores

Power on VM

Confirm OS boot, network connectivity, and disk integrity

Application/database restores

Confirm services start cleanly

Run basic queries or application health checks

Validate logs (DB consistency checks where applicable)

User/Application Validation

Work with application owners to:

Confirm data usability

Validate business functionality

For DR tests:

Confirm application dependencies and integration points

4. Documentation, Reporting & Feedback Loop

Post-restore activities improve future success.

Document restore details

Backup set used

Restore duration vs RTO

Any errors or manual intervention

Update runbooks

Add lessons learned

Adjust restore procedures or scripts

Recommend improvements

Backup frequency adjustments

Infrastructure tuning (network, storage, dedupe)

Agent or configuration changes

Key Principles I Follow

Assume backups are untrusted until restored

Test restores regularly, not just during incidents

Tier 1 systems get scheduled restore tests

Validation is both technical and business-facing
